                {
                    new TheorySection { heading = "Collective", body = "The collective lever changes the pitch of all main rotor blades together. More pitch means more thrust, and more power needed to keep the rotor turning at 100%. Raise it to climb, lower it to descend. In the Kestrel the governor adds engine power automatically, but it cannot exceed what the engine can give: over-pitch and the rotor RPM droops." },
                    new TheorySection { heading = "Cyclic", body = "The cyclic tilts the rotor disc. The thrust tilts with it, so the helicopter accelerates in the direction the disc is tilted: forward cyclic lowers the nose and moves you forward. The aircraft has inertia, so it keeps moving until you tilt the disc the other way. Lead your corrections and keep them small." },
                    new TheorySection { heading = "Pedals and torque", body = "Driving the main rotor twists the fuselage the opposite way. The tail rotor counters this torque; the pedals change its thrust to control heading. Every time you change power with the collective, the torque changes and the nose wants to swing, so collective and pedals move together." },
                    new TheorySection { heading = "Ground effect and translational lift", body = "Within about one rotor diameter of the ground the downwash is cushioned and less power is needed to hover: in ground effect (IGE). As you accelerate through 12 to 20 kt the rotor moves into undisturbed air and becomes markedly more efficient: effective translational lift (ETL). You feel a brief shudder, then the aircraft wants to climb." },
                },
                quiz = new[]
